 "rule": "br-kp1in.40 closing re-measurement (written 2026-10-14 ~07:20Z, before the landing push, before any build or run of B).\n\nQuestion: with br-qctx4 (direct inbox read single-table/rowid-driven), br-kp1in.40's probe (no shared-mailbox join for a\nproject without deliveries) and, if it lands, br-g11iy (no write transaction for already-read rows) on main, are inbox\nreads at equal send rate still slower than shipped v0.3.38?\n\nSame experiment and thresholds as kp140_attr_rule.txt (which found v0.3.39 READS SLOWER: 0.834x reads, p50 1.19x,\np99 1.24x):\n- mixed_rate.py, 2 senders A->B paced at 4 sends/s total (open loop), 2 closed-loop readers fetch_inbox(B, limit 20,\n  mark_read=false), 1 search loop, 1 Hz health, 300 s. Order A B B A, host lock, nothing else of mine on the host.\n- A = shipped v0.3.38 glibc artifact (am 171713fa, scratchpad build33/v038/am), as in kp140_attr.\n- B = main at the pushed landing commit, built with run_release_build4.sh (dist features: --no-default-features\n  --features portable), am SHA recorded per run.\n- Validity: every run completes >= 1,140 of 1,200 scheduled sends.\n\nDecision:\n  READS NOT SLOWER: pooled B read count >= 0.90 x A AND pooled B read p99 <= 1.5 x A. Then br-kp1in.40's regression is\n    resolved at equal offered load: the bead closes with this evidence plus kp140_attr's attribution (the closed-loop\n    soak still measures send-rate-driven inbox growth; that harness property is reported, not chased).\n  READS SLOWER: either half fails. Then the bead stays open with the per-window read p50 vs sends_before for both arms,\n    and the next attribution step (evidence-ledger phases) runs before any further code change.\nReported, not deciding: Mode R (mark_read=true) A B B A on the same two binaries; read p50; send p50/p99.\n",
